# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2.3 have "offended_by_arbitrary" and "offended_by_arbitrary → evil_names_to_superiors" and "¬arbitrary_government → war_perpetual" and "force_from_arms"; hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2.2 has "offended_by_arbitrary" and "offended_by_arbitrary → evil_names_to_superiors" and "□(¬arbitrary_government → war_perpetual)" and "force_from_arms"; they differ when only "that which offendeth the People is no other thing but that they are governed as the Publique Representant thinks fit, that is, by an Arbitrary government", "they give evill names to their Superiors", "there is such Arbitrary government", "Warre must be perpetuall" and "it is Men and Arms, not Words and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws" are true, which the first allows
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2.3 with no modality; hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "that which offendeth the People is no other thing but that they are governed as the Publique Representant thinks fit, that is, by an Arbitrary government", "they give evill names to their Superiors", "there is such Arbitrary government", "Warre must be perpetuall" and "it is Men and Arms, not Words and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws" are true and "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is such Arbitrary government, then Warre must be perpetuall)" is false. Is S5, "And that which offendeth the People, is no other thing, but that they are governed, not as every one of them would himselfe, but as the Publique Representant, be it one Man, or an Assembly of men thinks fit; that is, by an Arbitrary government: for which they give evill names to their Superiors; never knowing (till perhaps a little after a Civill warre) that without such Arbitrary government, such Warre must be perpetuall; and that it is Men, and Arms, not Words, and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/negation: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2 P3 (S3): the sentence says "no", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "afterwards in most parts of Greece that kind of government (monarchy) was abolished"
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/negation: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r2 P4 (S3): the sentence says "no", but P4 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if afterwards in most parts of Greece that kind of government (monarchy) was abolished, then the name Tyrant began to signifie, with the thing it did before, the hatred which the Popular States bare towards it"
